<h2>Where the provision sits in the appellate scheme</h2>
<p>Appeals against acquittal are themselves exceptional. The State needs leave under Section 419 BNSS; a complainant in a complaint case needs special leave; and appellate courts interfere with acquittals only on compelling grounds, because the trial court&rsquo;s verdict fortifies the presumption of innocence. Section 431 is the liberty-management clause of this exceptional jurisdiction: once such an appeal is on file, the court must be able to ensure the respondent&rsquo;s availability without treating him as a convict — which he is not.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Appeal presented.</strong> The State&rsquo;s appeal (with leave) or complainant&rsquo;s appeal (with special leave) against acquittal is admitted.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Process issues.</strong> The High Court may direct a warrant — in practice usually bailable — requiring the acquitted person to appear before it or a subordinate court.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Liberty decided.</strong> The court before which he appears either admits him to bail on bonds or, in rare cases, commits him to custody pending the appeal.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Appeal decided.</strong> On dismissal, the bonds stand discharged; on reversal, the respondent surrenders or is taken into custody to serve the sentence imposed.</div>
</div>

<h2>How courts exercise the discretion</h2>
<p>The jurisprudence under the predecessor provision settled a humane default: a person who has earned an acquittal after trial is ordinarily continued on bail, and committal to prison pending an acquittal appeal is reserved for genuinely exceptional situations. Factors that matter include the gravity of the charge and the sentence that would follow reversal; the respondent&rsquo;s conduct during trial — attendance, compliance, absence of tampering allegations; roots in the community and flight risk; and the apparent strength of the appeal. Courts are also conscious of timing: acquittal appeals often pend for years, and committing a presumptively innocent person for that span would punish him for the prosecution&rsquo;s optimism.</p>

<h2>Practical guidance for the acquitted respondent</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Treat the notice seriously: non-appearance converts a routine bond matter into a non-bailable warrant problem. Appear on the first date, through counsel and in person where directed.</p>
  <p>Come prepared with sureties: courts typically dispose of the Section 431 stage in one hearing when bonds and local sureties are ready.</p>
  <p>Keep addresses updated with the court; most custody complications in acquittal appeals begin with unserved notices.</p>
  <p>Remember that the acquittal remains fully operative during the appeal — a point with civil consequences for employment and otherwise — unless and until it is reversed.</p>
</div>

<h2>For the appellant&rsquo;s side</h2>
<p>Prosecutors and complainants should invoke Section 431 purposively, not punitively. Seeking custody of the respondent at the admission stage, without case-specific justification, usually fails and sours the bench; what the appeal actually needs is a secured presence, which bonds provide. Where genuine flight risk exists — respondents with pending extradition histories, or those who absconded during trial — the material should be placed on affidavit, since the committal power exists precisely for such cases.</p>
<div class="note">This explainer describes the general statutory scheme for information. The exercise of discretion under Section 431 BNSS depends entirely on the facts of each appeal.</div>
